# Conclusion

This study presented a reproducible computational workflow for analysing psychotic discourse through the interaction between meta-communication and delusional ontology, operationalized as paragraph-level textual features and examined across extended narrative material. Applied to Schreber's *Memoirs of My Nervous Illness*, the analysis did not support models of continuous escalation or stable structural coupling. Instead, it revealed a weakly organized global structure punctuated by localized peaks of intensified interaction, alongside limited temporal persistence and modest but significant lagged dependencies.

These findings have direct theoretical implications for the understanding of psychosis. In particular, they suggest not that phenomenological accounts of altered subjectivity are invalid, but that, at the level of discursive organization, their expression in the present operationalization and corpus appears episodic rather than uniformly distributed. The classical phenomenological tradition — from Minkowski's (1927) morbid rationalism and Blankenburg's (1969/2001) loss of natural self-evidence to Kimura's (2001) account of persistent self-splitting and Parnas and Sass's (2001) ipseity-disturbance model — consistently treats the schizophrenic alteration as a continuous structural condition rather than an intermittent state. While Sass's (1994b, 1994c; Sass & Byrom, 2015) concept of hyperreflexivity remains a powerful descriptive framework, the present results indicate that its observable manifestations in discourse may be episodic rather than uniformly distributed. Psychotic discourse, in this sense, appears less as a continuously transformed system and more as a temporally heterogeneous field in which moments of intensified coupling emerge against a background of relative structural looseness.

This does not invalidate phenomenological models; rather, it makes selected aspects of their discursive implications empirically tractable and open to specification. By translating constructs such as meta-communication and ontological elaboration into measurable features, the present approach enables a shift from purely interpretative accounts to models that can be systematically tested, compared, and refined. In doing so, it aligns with broader efforts in computational psychiatry to formalize complex psychological phenomena while preserving their clinical and experiential relevance (Huys et al., 2016).

More broadly, the findings support a view of psychotic discourse as a dynamic and non-uniform process, characterised by transient regimes of organization rather than stable global structures. This perspective challenges models that assume sustained coherence or continuous inferential integration and instead emphasizes variability, contingency, and temporal modulation as central features. In this respect, the study contributes to an emerging dialogue between phenomenology, cognitive theory, and computational modelling, demonstrating that even richly interpreted clinical texts can be re-examined as structured data without reducing their conceptual depth.

Future work may extend this approach by applying the workflow to additional corpora, refining the operationalization of discursive features, and integrating more formal computational models. In particular, the identification of episodic coupling patterns opens the possibility of developing explicitly dynamic models of psychosis, in which shifts between discursive regimes can be simulated and empirically evaluated. Such developments would not only deepen our understanding of psychotic phenomena but also help establish a common analytical language across traditionally distinct paradigms.

In this sense, the present study demonstrates that the episodic structure of psychotic discourse can be formally modelled through the dynamic interaction between meta-communication and delusional ontology, establishing a reproducible framework for analysing how meaning is intermittently organized in extreme forms of human experience.
